{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    // `worker-configuration.d.ts` is generated by `npm run types`, and it is the
    // Workers runtime library. It replaced `@cloudflare/workers-types`, which
    // wrangler 4.123 supersedes and says so on every `wrangler types` run.
    //
    // The improvement is that the types now come from the installed workerd and
    // this repo's own `compatibility_date` rather than from whatever version of a
    // separate package happened to resolve — one fewer thing that can disagree
    // with the runtime the specs actually boot. It also ends a collision the last
    // `@cloudflare/workers-types` bump introduced: that package declared a global
    // `const Buffer: any`, which degraded `@types/node`'s `Buffer` in both
    // meanings at once and forced a hand-written re-typing in `src/testing/vcr*`.
    //
    // Generated with `--include-env=false`, and that flag is load-bearing rather
    // than tidiness. A published package cannot reference a consumer's ambient
    // `Env`; the default output declares a global `Env` built from *this* repo's
    // dev-only `wrangler.jsonc` bindings, so including it would make a stray
    // `Env` reference typecheck here and then resolve to a consumer's bindings at
    // their build, where the mistake is invisible. Without it there is no global
    // `Env` to reach for and the mistake fails here — the property the old
    // `@cloudflare/workers-types` entry got by leaving this file out entirely.
    // `npm run types:check` is what keeps the flag honest; see
    // `scripts/verify-runtime-types.mjs`.
    //
    // The one permitted exception is the *namespaced* `Cloudflare.Env`, which is
    // not the same thing: the runtime types declare it as an empty
    // `interface Env {}` that `wrangler types` augments in the consumer, and the
    // Agents SDK constrains its own base class to it
    // (`class Agent<Env extends Cloudflare.Env = Cloudflare.Env>`). So
    // `RecipeSubagentBase` in `src/subagent/index.ts` must mirror that
    // constraint — dropping it fails with "Type 'TEnv' does not satisfy the
    // constraint 'Env'". Bare `Env` stays forbidden; `Cloudflare.Env` is a
    // declaration-merging seam, not a binding of this repo's.
    //
    // A path rather than a package name, which `types` accepts: this is a file in
    // the repo, not a package under `node_modules/@types`.
    //
    // Committed, despite being generated. It is an input to `tsc` and to eslint's
    // type-aware pass, so leaving it untracked means a fresh clone cannot
    // typecheck until someone runs a script — and an editor opened before that
    // shows a few thousand phantom errors. `npm run types:check` fails `check`
    // when the committed copy no longer matches `wrangler.jsonc` and the
    // installed workerd, which are its only two inputs. Regenerate with
    // `npm run types` and commit the result.
    //
    // `@cloudflare/vitest-plugin/types` is here for `src/testing/*`, which
    // imports `cloudflare:test`. That subpath is only ever loaded from a test
    // run, and a consumer of `@dynamicagents/core/testing` has the plugin installed
    // by definition — nothing in the runtime graph reaches it.
    "types": [
      "./worker-configuration.d.ts",
      "@cloudflare/vitest-plugin/types",
      "node"
    ],
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "isolatedModules": true
  },
  // Everything under `src/`, specs included. Specs are deliberately NOT excluded
  // here: eslint's type-aware pass resolves each file to its *nearest* tsconfig,
  // so a co-located spec excluded from this project has no project at all and
  // fails to parse. `tsconfig.build.json` is where specs get held out of `dist`.
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"]
}
